<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01974</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fc696584-2dd3-4c51-b69d-0c1b355a48e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00001">For 1 - 4, use the following information:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00002"><quote id="quote-00001">A parabola crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at (-1,0) and (2,0). We want to write a quadratic equation with those <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts. We can do this without graphing.</quote></para><para id="para-00003">When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, what must <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00005">We know that (-1,0) and (2,0) are points on the graph when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. So there are two possibilities for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
    
    <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> are called the real solutions of the quadratic because they are the solutions of the equation when we set <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00007">Rewrite the equations from number 1 so they both equal zero.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00009"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
    
    <para id="para-00010">Because each of the equations equals zero, multiplying them together will also equal zero.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00011">What equation do you get when you multiply the previous equations together?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00013"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00014">Multiply the binomials.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00016"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
    
    <para id="para-00017">This answer can also give us the more general quadratic with those <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts. We replace 0 with <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to show that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> varies and isn’t always 0. So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is a parabola that crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at -1 and 2.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
</section>

<note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
  <para id="para-00018">The solution to a quadratic equation is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. What is the equation?</para>
</note>

<section id="sect-00002"><para id="para-00019">When you write a quadratic in factored form, that helps you find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts. 
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
</para>
  <para id="para-00020">When we set <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, we find the places where the parabola crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00021"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
  
  <para id="para-00022">So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and 1.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00023">-3 and 1 are called the real solutions to the quadratic equation.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00024">We can also start with the real solutions and use them to write quadratic equations.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00025">When we know that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and 1, then that means <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00026">So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00027">And, using the FOIL method to multiply this out, we come to 
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
  
  <para id="para-00028">When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is not equal to zero, we have the following quadratic equation</para>
  
  <para id="para-00029"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, which is exactly where we started!</para>

  
  <!-- BEGIN TRY IT-->

  <note class="try" id="note-00002">
<title>Try It: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
<!--Q#1-->
<exercise id="exer-00005">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
  
    <para id="para-00030">Write a quadratic from the real solutions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> without graphing.</para>
  <para id="para-00031">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
<para id="para-00032">Compare your answer: 
<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>

<para id="para-00033"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> become <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

</solution></exercise></note>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TRY IT --></section>
</content></document>
